<author id="732669">Marshall</author>
<doctext field_name="doctext">Yes, there appears to be quite a controversy about what &lt;c&gt;print "\n";&lt;/c&gt; means when using the defaults on various platforms.&lt;p&gt;

Perl's behaviour for me is easy to understand for write: a)Old Mac &lt;CR&gt;, b) Unix &lt;LF&gt;, c) Windows &lt;CR&gt;&lt;LF&gt;.&lt;p&gt;

I believe that when writing to a network socket, &lt;CR&gt;&lt;LF&gt; is used, whether a), b), or c).&lt;p&gt;

For the most part Perl hides these details (except when it doesn't).&lt;br&gt;
That there is such controversy about such a seemingly simple thing implies that a FAQ is needed.
</doctext>
